"""Document paired finite-event detector redesign with negative results."""
import json,sys
from pathlib import Path
from docx import Document
from docx.oxml import OxmlElement
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1];sys.path.insert(0,str(ROOT/'experiments'));import paper50 as P
p=ROOT/'paper/MEGA27-23b-50p.docx';d=Document(p);title='Full Fletcher fixes the single-event misses but not the double-event mechanism'
assert not any(x.text==title for x in d.paragraphs)
j=json.loads((ROOT/'results/block_checksum_upgrade_audit.json').read_text())
P.h1(d,title)
P.para(d,'The reachable low-byte misses suggest an immediate detector redesign: keep both Fletcher bytes rather than only the low one. We tested this idea in an isolated audit candidate, not by changing the production codec. The candidate appends all twelve check trits to each fixed 32-trit block, increasing length from 38 to 44 bases. The same eight original payload blocks and initial seeds are used. Payload prefixes are byte-for-byte identical between candidates, while the six additional check bases change the suffix.')
P.para(d,'To make the comparison paired, both candidates receive exactly the same changed positions and alternative letters within the 32-base payload prefix. We enumerate 96 single and 4,464 double events per block, for 768 and 35,712 events across eight blocks. The six or twelve check-region bases are not changed. This narrower library permits a detector comparison without treating the longer check region as additional physical error exposure. It cannot establish whole-file recovery or an equal-budget comparison. The original 38-base all-position enumeration remains separate.')
P.table(d,'Paired payload-position events. Identical payload prefixes and mutations; different check lengths.',['Outcome','Low-byte single','Full single','Low-byte double','Full double'],[['Spacing rejection','417','417','27,955','27,956'],['Checksum rejection','66','351','2,615','6,103'],['Accepted wrong','285','0','5,142','1,653'],['Total','768','768','35,712','35,712']])
P.h2(d,'The negative result is a structured collision, not residual random chance')
P.para(d,'All 285 single-event low-byte misses become checksum rejections under full Fletcher in this library. Among double events, 3,489 low-byte accepted wrong payloads become full-check rejections, but 1,653 remain accepted wrong under both candidates. Thus the full-check accepted-wrong fraction is about 4.63% of the paired double-event library, rather than zero. This cannot be interpreted as a physical error probability or a universal worst-case bound. Sequence choices, mutation count and selected positions are fixed by the enumeration.')
P.para(d,'The retained double-event counterexample changes base zero from T to G and base two from G to T in block zero. Its first payload trits change from [2,1,0,1] to [1,2,1,0]. The two local compensating pairs preserve total trit sum, and their weighted effects on the high Fletcher accumulator cancel each other. Both stored checksum bytes therefore agree despite a different payload. The new regression test explicitly requires two changed DNA positions, different decoded payloads, equal full Fletcher checksums and acceptance by the audit candidate. Merely retaining the high byte does not guarantee detection of two DNA substitutions.')
P.para(d,'Paired spacing-rejection counts need not agree exactly, even though the payload prefixes match. A change at the last payload position changes the transition into the first checksum base, whose value differs between candidates. The joint outcome ledger records two single and 89 double events exchanging spacing and checksum rejection across candidates. These are rejected in either case, but explain why a candidate-length change can alter the diagnostic category without changing payload mutations. It would be wrong to force identical category totals or silently discard seam effects.')
P.h2(d,'Charge the extra detector before proposing an advantage')
P.para(d,'Six extra bases per block increase isolated block length by 15.79%. With 32 payload trits, six trits per payload byte and three identical copies, the asymptotic payload rate would fall from 0.374269 to 0.323232 bits per encoded nucleotide if this detector replaced every current block check. Headers, parity blocks and finite-size rounding would further affect the exact rate. This is a proposed architecture calculation, not a production encoding measurement: the current codec remains unchanged. Primers, indexing and physical molecular copies remain outside this software base accounting.')
P.para(d,'The experiment supports retaining the full checksum as a stronger detector in the tested single-event library, but shows that this alone does not remove structured double-event misses. A next design should choose an error-detecting or correcting code with stated distance properties for the actual differential channel, account for its full strand geometry, and test complete-file recovery under clean and strict noisy channels. Trading bases for stronger detection may reduce outer parity or copy budget. Those coupled choices need a new matched experiment, rather than declaring success because one counterexample disappears.')
P.h2(d,'Reproduction and limits of the candidate')
P.para(d,'Run python3 experiments/block_checksum_upgrade_audit.py. The paired ledger records block, event order, positions, alternative letters, and both detector outcomes for all 36,480 events. The summary retains exact joint counts, same-prefix assertion, geometry, rate calculation and the full-check collision. Tests validate the ledger digest and totals and independently verify the double-DNA collision. The SHA256 below is split into two lines only for display.')
P.para(d,j['trial_sha256'][:32]+'\n'+j['trial_sha256'][32:])
P.para(d,'Only payload-region substitutions are enumerated. Mutated check bases, indels, dropout, replicate disagreement, whole-message checksum interactions, parity repair, strand constraints and synthesis are not measured here. No candidate is installed in the production encoder, and no successful complete-file improvement is claimed. The finite paired negative is retained because it rejects the tempting inference that full Fletcher turns every low-byte miss into a guaranteed erasure.')
for row in d.tables[-1].rows:row._tr.get_or_add_trPr().append(OxmlElement('w:cantSplit'))
P.save(d,str(p));print('appended paired detector upgrade audit')
